WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:03.780
Most AI projects start from an empty repository.

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.784
With AI App Server, you start from a working app.

3
00:00:07.164 --> 00:00:14.417
In this video: the ten built-in apps, the three commands that deploy one, and the tests that let you change it with confidence.

4
00:00:15.497 --> 00:00:17.097
Here's what ships in the box.

5
00:00:18.177 --> 00:00:21.143
There are ten blueprints, each a complete, tested app.

6
00:00:21.523 --> 00:00:29.672
Knowledge chat answers from your documents, with citations. Customer support calls your tools, and asks a person before refunds.

7
00:00:30.052 --> 00:00:41.145
Document extraction turns invoices and forms into JSON, with the page and quote for every field. Summarization and classification handle long documents and routing.

8
00:00:41.525 --> 00:00:52.619
Meeting notes, content review with human approval, data insights over SQL and CSV, a code assistant, and a workflow agent with tools and MCP complete the set.

9
00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:54.787
Let's build one.

10
00:00:55.167 --> 00:00:58.111
aiapp new creates the app, and prints the next steps.

11
00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:09.477
Inside: the config file, sample documents, tests, golden evaluations, and instructions that teach coding assistants such as GitHub Copilot how the SDK works.

12
00:01:09.857 --> 00:01:15.212
Add your documents, deploy, and the app is live, with a chat page and a streaming API.

13
00:01:16.292 --> 00:01:21.049
This is the built-in chat page, answering from the employee handbook, with its source.

14
00:01:21.429 --> 00:01:25.632
It ran on a local model on a CPU, so no data left the machine.

15
00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:28.696
Customizing is mostly settings.

16
00:01:29.076 --> 00:01:33.343
This workflow agent's instructions are plain text in its config file.

17
00:01:33.723 --> 00:01:38.096
Its step limit, and the tools that wait for a person's approval, are settings too.

18
00:01:38.476 --> 00:01:41.825
And tools from MCP servers plug in with one line.

19
00:01:42.905 --> 00:01:48.815
When you need code, add a handler in Python or JavaScript, for just that route, job or event.

20
00:01:49.195 --> 00:01:52.736
The server starts, watches and restarts the workers that run it.

21
00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:55.523
Every blueprint ships with tests.

22
00:01:55.903 --> 00:02:02.900
aiapp test starts a throwaway server and replaces the models with scripts, so tests are fast, free and repeatable.

23
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.443
Everything else is real: routes, workers, storage, search, jobs and guardrails.

24
00:02:08.823 --> 00:02:17.164
aiapp eval runs golden questions against your real models, checking content, citations, latency, and a model's judgement.

25
00:02:17.544 --> 00:02:21.512
Put both in CI, and you catch regressions before your users do.

26
00:02:22.592 --> 00:02:27.477
Every deploy is a new, immutable version, and switches over with zero downtime.

27
00:02:27.857 --> 00:02:29.649
Here, version two goes live.

28
00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:31.864
aiapp versions lists them all.

29
00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:34.911
And if something's wrong, rollback is one command.

30
00:02:35.991 --> 00:02:37.655
And it runs where your data is.

31
00:02:38.035 --> 00:02:45.843
There are installers for Windows, macOS and Linux, a Docker image, a Helm chart for Kubernetes, and templates for Azure.

32
00:02:46.223 --> 00:02:53.945
The VS Code extension creates apps from blueprints, runs tests, deploys and rolls back, and checks your config as you type.

33
00:02:54.325 --> 00:02:58.144
From idea to production in minutes, on infrastructure you control.

34
00:02:59.224 --> 00:03:02.488
That's the end of this four-part series. Thanks for watching.

35
00:03:02.868 --> 00:03:09.823
AI App Server is free to use, and its SDKs, example apps and templates are Apache 2.0.

36
00:03:10.203 --> 00:03:16.731
Download it and follow the ten-minute quickstart at github.com/Sandaal/ai-app-server.

